    author "dk"
}

record lege-ecosystem-perf.renderer-headsup.round3-merged-20261104/1 : observation {
    title "Round 3 merged: Lege ahead of Hayro on 7/8 documents serially, bit-identical; render crates 0.4.0, jp2lam 0.3.1, jbig2enc-rust 0.6.1"
    state verified
    statement """
        Round 3 (three bounded Opus workers, merged as d9c6d147 / a15c7c45 / 2503eece) keeps the renderer bit-identical
        on 3698/3698 corpus page renders and, serially at 144 DPI P-core pinned, puts Lege ahead of Hayro 0.9.0 on 7 of 8
        documents: Structures 1.66x, xenosystems 1.65x, chinese-medicine 1.46x, beans 1.36x, centralpark 1.30x, sahib
        1.29x, vogtle 1.09x, viaduct 0.99x (tie). Remaining losses: beans at 72 DPI 0.77x (fixed per-page cost, glyph-cache
        misses) and vogtle at 8 workers 0.62x (22 pages; Lege scales 2.2x vs Hayro 3.1x, a pipeline ramp question).
        Levers landed: whole-glyph blend_mask_rect with in-kernel coverage count, solid fills from scanline runs, cached
        Indexed expansion, exact row box filter and uniform-mask shortcuts for masked area-min, windowed JBIG2 generic
        decoder (Structures p0 mask 28.8 -> 7.8 ms). Corrections to earlier records: viaduct is three JPEG images, not
        vector; the Structures JBIG2 layer is a 1-bit /SMask already on the masked fast path. Versions: render crates
        0.4.0 (explicit, CHANGELOG added), jp2lam 0.3.1, jbig2enc-rust 0.6.1. Suites: 291 + 270 + the real-stream
        equivalence pass; the six tests in jbig2enc-rust/tests/roundtrip_after_fix.rs need an external jbig2dec binary
        absent on this host. Evidence: .agent/scratch/pdf-render-headsup-20261104/variance-investigation.md section 11,
        variance-investigation/round3/, and the dated notes in dev-notes/lege-pdf/render-refinement/performance-history/.
        """
    observed_at git:58e7d7de4f41392c786976fd76fdf89edbc64c75
    method command
    watches [
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-cpu/src/exec.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-cpu/src/kernels.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-cpu/src/raster.rs",
        "lege-codecs/jbig2enc-rust/src/decode/generic.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/CHANGELOG.md",
        "lege-pdf/CRATES-IO-PLAN.md"
    ]
    author "dk"
}

record lege-ecosystem-perf.renderer-headsup.round4-followups-20261104/1 : observation {
    title "Round 4 follow-ups: scheduler window, font dedup, adaptive flattening, masked alpha pass, low-bit-depth image path; halftone/HENSKIP tested"
    state verified
    statement """
        Round 4 (2026-11-04, main checkout, no worktrees or implementation subagents, no version change; commit e7d1b298eba07cac3f8da5ea8a0ba0514f7cdf54) took the
        round-3 "next levers" one at a time. (1) Short-document ramp: the scheduler's fixed reorder window of 12 starved the render
        workers on short documents; the default is now reorder_window_for(compile, render) = 4 slots per worker, floor 12, with the
        page feed sized to it, and the document font cache resolves each font once across compile workers (per-font slot with an
        in-flight lock). vogtle at 8 workers: 0.62x -> level with hayro (best of three 45 -> 38 ms vs 39). (2) Glyph-cache misses:
        outline curves are flattened adaptively (fewest uniform segments within 1/16 px, FreeType's threshold, cap 64) instead of a
        fixed 8 per curve; beans serial 72 DPI 22.7 -> 14.7 ms (0.77x -> 1.31x hayro). This is the one output change: 2-7% of text
        pixels move by 4 levels on average, max 28; PDF_RENDERER_OUTLINE_SEGMENTS=8 reproduces the old output byte for byte and the
        corpus hash check runs under it (3698/3698 vs 035660da). (3) Masked area-min alpha pass skips clear runs: Structures' masked
        draw 7.7 -> 7.3 ms per page, exact; the AVX2 horizontal pass from the hand-off profiled at 2% and was not built. (4) JBIG2
        halftone, tested not changed: a halftone-region PDF made with Lege 1.5.1 (--halftone --text-format jbig2, viaduct; HENSKIP=0,
        Lege's encoder never sets it) and synthetic HENSKIP=1 streams all decode identically to hayro; the one defect was in
        jbig2enc-rust's test-only stream writer, which fed skipped cells' values into later contexts (T.88 6.2.5.7 has 0), fixed
        in tests/common/writer.rs; the decoder is unchanged. (5) Found by that test: 1-, 2- and 4-bit single-component images drawn
        axis-aligned at or above their resolution (Lege's own JBIG2/CCITT pages) had no fast path and took the generic per-pixel
        loop at ~30 ns a pixel; the new lookup-table nearest painter is byte-identical (unit test; 3740/3740 hashes vs the
        pre-change adapter) and brings Lege's own output from 105 to 5.5 ms per page at 144 DPI (hayro 8.3) and 27.5 to 1.9 ms at
        72 DPI (hayro 3.9); the halftone pages 175-205 -> 9-10 ms. JPEG Huffman and page composition examined, unchanged.
        Timing drive (round-4 vs round-3 adapter vs hayro 0.9.0, one run, P-core pinned), serial 144 DPI lege/hayro:
        Structures 1.67x, beans 1.41x, centralpark 1.48x, chinese-medicine 1.39x, sahib 1.38x, viaduct 1.06x, vogtle 1.04x, xenosystems 1.61x; serial 72 DPI: Structures 1.53x, beans 1.09x, centralpark 1.78x, chinese-medicine 1.33x, sahib 1.88x, viaduct 1.07x, vogtle 1.00x, xenosystems 1.73x; 8 workers 144 DPI: Structures 1.80x, beans 1.27x, centralpark 1.28x, chinese-medicine 1.74x, sahib 1.15x, viaduct 1.07x, vogtle 0.91x, xenosystems 1.44x. Suites 292 + 360, clippy 0 errors. Evidence:
        .agent/scratch/pdf-render-headsup-20261104/variance-investigation.md section 12, variance-investigation/round4/ (incl.
        halftone/ with the Lege-made PDFs, the HENSKIP streams and the scanner), and
        dev-notes/lege-pdf/render-refinement/performance-history/optimization-round4-followups-20261104.md.
        """
    observed_at git:e7d1b298eba07cac3f8da5ea8a0ba0514f7cdf54
    method manual
    watches [
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-scheduler/src/lib.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-content/src/interpret.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-cpu/src/exec.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-cpu/src/prepared.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-cpu/src/image.rs",
        "lege-codecs/jbig2enc-rust/src/decode/halftone_region.rs"
    ]
    author "dk"
}

record lege-ecosystem-perf.renderer-headsup.timing-variance-20261104/1 : observation {
    title "Nov-04 renderer head-to-head timing variance explained: protocol change plus JPX full-resolution decode"
    state verified
    statement """
        The 2026-11-04 renderer head-to-head timings differ from the July/August pdfium-diff bench results for three
        methodological reasons and one real engine cost. (1) July measured Lege on 20 threads with nested JPX/image-row
        parallelism in a pre-warmed process against single-threaded PDFium; November is 8-vs-8 with nested parallelism
        off, cold processes, and PDFium in 8 processes (5.7x its serial rate). (2) The serial dump pass ran Lege with
        PDF_RENDERER_JPX_CONCURRENCY=1 (unrecognised = Budgeted(8)) while timing used serial: 92 vs 195 ms/page on
        chinese-medicine 72 DPI. (3) No CPU affinity on the i7-13700H: pinned P-cores 178-181 ms/page, E-cores 293,
        and one unpinned full run landed at 315; single-pass serial numbers carry a 1.6x error bar. Cold start adds
        130-480 ms (system font directory scan of 535 files plus first-page caches), which is the whole result for the
        3-page documents. (4) The real cost: on the JPX/MRC books the renderer is 13-23 ms/page warm but jp2lam decodes
        both full-page JPX layers at full resolution at 144 DPI (SUPERSAMPLE_HEADROOM=2 in pdf-render-cpu prepared.rs
        rejects the one-level reduction), 300-380 ms per layer serial; Lege peaks at ~11 pages/s with the whole machine
        vs PDFium 30 pages/s with 8 processes. Report and raw data:
        .agent/scratch/pdf-render-headsup-20261104/variance-investigation.md and variance-investigation/.
        """
    observed_at git:279debcc0b71e61bf495e71f12c89991883c3323
    method command
    watches [
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-render-cpu/src/prepared.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-image/src/jpx.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/crates/pdf-font/src/system.rs",
        "lege-pdf/render/README.md"
    ]
    author "dk"
}
